    "https://jobs.sandiego.edu/jobs/open-rank-assistant-associate-or-full-professor-of-english-and-writing-program-director-san-diego-california-united-states": {
      "title": "Open Rank (Assistant, Associate or Full) Professor of English and Writing Program Director",
      "description": "Open Rank (Assistant, Associate or Full) Professor of English and Writing Program Director - San Diego, California, United States Skip to main content Why USD Benefits and Compensation Already Applied? My Profile Kiosk mode Employment Opportunities at USD Open Rank (Assistant, Associate or Full) Professor of English and Writing Program Director San Diego, California Faculty Full-time regular Closing on: Nov 15 2026 5668 Position Title & Department: Open Rank (Assistant, Associate or Full) Professor of English and Writing Program Director Posting # 5668 Department Description: The Department of English at the University of San Diego, housed within the College of Artsand Sciences, is a multidisciplinary Department that features courses in domestic and global literatures, literary cultures and theories, and creative writing. The Department also houses the University’s First Year Writing Program. The College of Arts and Sciences is at the heart of the University of San Diego. Our philosophy is built on the enduring values of the Catholic intellectual tradition, immersing students in the concepts of free inquiry, ethical conduct and compassionate service. The College aims to establish an academic foundation for students to thrive and gain a comprehensive education, which spans the sciences, arts and social sciences. Through our exemplary liberal arts education and rigorous scholarship, we aspire to not only educate the mind but also develop the whole person. University Description: The University of San Diego, an engaged and contemporary Catholic institution, was founded by the Diocese of San Diego and the Society of the Sacred Heart in 1949. Governed by an independent board of trustees since 1972, USD remains committed to a liberal arts education grounded in the Catholic intellectual tradition and the pursuit of truth, goodness and beauty. Inspired by this centuries old tradition of Catholic higher education, the University welcomes people of all faith traditions and any, or no, religious background. The future success of USD relies on the contributions of those who seek to foster the development of engaged global citizens and an earnest confrontation of humanity’s urgent challenges . Detailed Description: The Department of English at the University of San Diego invites applications for the position of Director of the University Writing Program to begin in Fall of 2027. We seek applicants at the level of advanced assistant, associate, or full professor of English. The teaching assignment is four courses per year. Teaching responsibilities include writing and literature courses at all levels. Administrative responsibilities include collaboration with faculty and administration in overseeing USD’s Writing Program; supervision of first-year writing courses; contributing to personnel decisions (in consultation with English department chair); incoming student assessment and competency exams; coordinating all upper division, cross-curricular writing courses, including training faculty, reviewing Core Advanced Writing-course applications and syllabi; offering training workshops for all Writing Program faculty; Writing Program assessment; oversight of English for Speakers of Other Languages student assessment and support programming. Job Requirements: The successful candidate will be a writing specialist with a PhD in English, Rhetoric and Composition, or a related field, with meaningful preparation in English literature preferred, and an interest in Creative Writing welcome. Substantial experience in Writing Program leadership administration, background in working with Writing Across the Curriculum and Writing in the Disciplines; Writing Program assessment; Writing Centers; and English for Speakers of Other Languages programs also required. Significant record of publication and research in the field expected. Background check: Successful completion of a pre-employment background check. Degree Verification Requirement : Persons offered employment in this position will be required to provide official education transcripts for degree verification purposes. Posting salary: $6,931.25-$11,666.67 monthly: Excellent Benefits The University of San Diego offers a very competitive benefits package including; medical, dental, vision, a retirement contribution given to you by the University, and access to on-campus Fitness Centers.  Please visit the benefits section of our website to view all of the perks and benefits that USD has to offer. USD: Human Resources: Benefits The salary range provided in this posting reflects what we reasonably expect to pay for this position.  Actual compensation offered or earned is dependent on experience, education and other factors including departmental budget. Special Application Instructions: Please submit all materials by Sunday, November 15, 2026 Application materials must include: A cover letter of application, which should express: A philosophy of writing pedagogy (particularly in an age of GenAI), including concrete examples of the ways that those principles have been implemented in teaching, research, and writing program administration. The candidate’s plans for service, commitment to the University mission, and inclusivity in teaching, research, and Writing Program Administration. Curriculum Vitae Research statement and sample of scholarly or, if applicable, creative work Teaching statement and sample syllabi that indicate an ability and willingness to teach reading and writing as twinned skills, as well as a discussion of the candidate’s commitment to inclusive pedagogy A one-page statement of vision for the writing program at USD Contact information for three references who will be sent a link to upload letters. Please contact Professor Brad Melekian, English Department Chair and Search Committee Chair, with any questions, at melekian@sandiego.edu . If you have any technical difficulties please contact the Employment Services Team at jobs@sandiego.edu. Additional Details: The University of San Diego is a smoking and tobacco-free campus. For more information, visit www.sandiego.edu/smokefree. Back to job search Recommended Jobs Cook San Diego, California Dining Services, Staff The University seeks an individual with a strong interest in working in a mission-driven, faith-based institution. The role of a Cook is working with the campus community and the responsibilities of the position are significantly tethered to the university’s contemporary Roman Catholic mission. Lead Cook - Asian Cuisine San Diego, California Dining Services, Staff The Lead Cook is responsible for aiding in the organization, coordination, and supervision of the preparation of hot food production, maintenance of quality and/or portion control, and the maintenance of sanitation and safety standards. The Lead Cook, under the supervision of the Chef Manager, acts as a working supervisor in training, coordinating, and evaluating the work of the lower-level cooks and other part time staff. Student Life Coordinator San Diego, California Staff, Student Affairs The Student Life Coordinator provides coordination of TRS and other student facing programs and administrative support to the Assistant Vice President for Student Life. Support includes managing calendars, incoming calls, email, and written correspondence as requested. Handles highly confidential information and materials. Facebook LinkedIn Instagram X © 2026 University of San Diego. All rights reserved. Cookie Preferences Web Privacy Web Accessibility Candidate Details We use cookies Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalise, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all"
    },
    "https://jobs.sandiego.edu/jobs/student-life-coordinator-san-diego-california-united-states": {
      "title": "Student Life Coordinator",
      "description": "Student Life Coordinator - San Diego, California, United States Skip to main content Why USD Benefits and Compensation Already Applied? My Profile Kiosk mode Employment Opportunities at USD Student Life Coordinator San Diego, California Full-time regular 5759 Position Title & Department: Student Life Coordinator; Student Life Posting # 5759 Department Description: In the Catholic tradition, the Student Affairs Division strives to create an inclusive, educational environment which motivates and supports student learning and personal development, serves the University community, and inspires students to make a positive contribution to society. Our philosophy and approach is to collaboratively work with Academic Affairs and all areas of the University to create an integrated learning environment. The Student Affairs Division is a dynamic, student centered and learning organization. We are poised to create a vibrant and active co-curricular learning experience for all students. Our emphasis on leadership development and social change allows us to work on developing future ethical leaders to serve in a complex, global and changing world. We are committed to fostering a sense of belonging and continuously building toward a just, welcoming and caring community for all. University Description: The University of San Diego, an engaged and contemporary Catholic institution, was founded by the Diocese of San Diego and the Society of the Sacred Heart in 1949. Governed by an independent board of trustees since 1972, USD remains committed to a liberal arts education grounded in the Catholic intellectual tradition and the pursuit of truth, goodness and beauty. Inspired by this centuries old tradition of Catholic higher education, the University welcomes people of all faith traditions and any, or no, religious background. The future success of USD relies on the contributions of those who seek to foster the development of engaged global citizens and an earnest confrontation of humanity’s urgent challenges . Detailed Description: The Student Life Coordinator provides coordination of TRS and other student facing programs and administrative support to the Assistant Vice President for Student Life. Support includes managing calendars, incoming calls, email, and written correspondence as requested. Handles highly confidential information and materials. Coordinates special projects including support for institutional assessment efforts and divisional strategic planning. Prepares financial documents and reconciles budget reports. Prepares materials and logistics for workshops, presentations, conferences, and committee meetings. Designs and produces electronic presentations, print materials, and informational handbooks. Manages development, production, and distribution of printed communications. Organizes various inter- and intra-departmental meetings and prepares agendas. Supports the VPSA Office, shares office management responsibilities. Periodically, this position may be asked to lead or contribute to either ad hoc or ongoing division/campus projects outside the scope of their day-to-day activities. Duties and Responsibilities: Primary Support for AVP Determines priority of issues for the AVP’s consideration; has all relevant materials available Reviews all incoming correspondence; forwards appropriate items and independently responds to inquiries Schedules appointments and maintains the calendars Composes correspondence and memoranda on behalf of the AVP, including highly confidential and sensitive material Serves as an information source for students, employees, parents, alumni, and the general public Screens and receives visitors, gives out information and/or refers to proper administrator/department and screens phone calls Maintains records/files for various committees and professional affiliations Prepares, organizes, manages and maintains files Arrange regular weekly and biweekly meetings for all directors reporting directly to the AVP. Assists with the coordination of all vacant position searches in the area as needed, including arranging search committee meetings, coordinating information distribution, contacting candidates, travel and meal accommodations, campus meetings, and notifications Scans and organizes all documents relating to the AVP electronically Is responsible for regular and special meeting preparations, including: Schedules meetings, reserves rooms/sets up virtual meeting spaces through Zoom, and invites participants Determines and requests catering and A/V needs Prepares agendas, binders, research, and other materials Identifies off-site meeting locations, reserves facilities, determines and coordinates food needs, and arranges payment Purchases supplies/equipment Obtains student representation as needed Torero Renaissance Scholars Coordination Supervise and coordinate the day-to-day operations of the Torero Renaissance Scholars (TRS) Program Develop, oversee and reconcile program/event budgets, ensuring the departments remain within the allocated budget. Assist in the tracking and reporting of TRS student participation, engagement with coaches, grades, etc. Assist the AVP for Student Life in coordinating and executing training and professional development opportunities for TRS Support Team. Hire and onboard the TRS Graduate Assistant Supervise the TRS Graduate Assistant, including meeting at least once a week. Communicates and works closely with TRS donors and University Advancement.  Keeps them regularly updated on what is occurring with TRS Helps to coordinate the Catalyst Grant and other grant opportunities for TRS students Communicate with students and/or campus partners about student’s needs. Discuss needs with AVP of Student Life Oversee the selection process for new TRS Scholars by working closely with Financial Aid Services Oversee the TRS orientation for all TRS Scholars Meet with TRS Scholars as needed Schedules and ensures that all new TRS Scholars have an initial one-on-one meeting with the AVP for Student Life Assist Graduate Assistant in the development and overall coordination of programming, including during nights and weekends, for TRS students; including but not limited to the following topics: Social Connection and Engagement Health and Wellness Financial Coaching and Assistance (FAFSA Workshop, Tax Filing) Career Readiness Cal-Fresh Enrollment Assistance Recruit and coordinate coaches for the TRS program Hold coaches meetings at least once per semester Coordinate campus cash for coaches so that they can take their scholar to coffee or lunch Coordinate TRS Scholar birthday recognitions Develop and Oversee the TRS Summer Intern Program Help with the annual grant reports Help look for new grant funding opportunities for TRS Provide TRS reports and updates to AVP of Student Life bi-monthly Conduce research on other private campus initiatives to support former foster youth and make recommendations as appropriate. Conduct other research as requested by the AVP for Student Life. Student in Needs Fund Coordination Communicate with student and/or campus partner about students’ needs. Discuss needs with AVP of Student Life Once support has been approved, facilitate distribution of funds as direct pay or through Financial Aid disbursement. Oversee accounts managing balance and expenditures. Provide monthly account reports to AVP of Student Life. Budget and Travel Maintains departmental budgets utilizing Workday, reconcile accounts, and prepares requisitions and other financial documents monthly Prepares various accounting online forms/reports Drafts and records Budget Appropriation Change and journal entry requests for AVP’s approval Coordinates AVP for Student Life’s travel/conference arrangements: hotel and airline reservations, conference and retreat payments/deposits, and transportation needs Reconciles One Card transaction records through Concur - Torero Travel and Expense. Other Administrative Duties Assists in and oversees preparation of bulk mailings (student surveys, etc.) Prepares special event arrangements, creates and sends invitations, maintains RSVP list, purchases supplies, makes room and catering arrangements as needed Assists the Vice President of Student Affairs and the AVP/Dean of Students in the absence of their respective assistants Supports the VPSA Office by sharing office management responsibilities Provide support for ongoing Student Affairs and Student Life programs, trainings, special events, and workshops Serve as committee member to regular working groups Serve as advisor to student organizations as needed Special Projects Identifies resources and conducts research via the Internet, library, calling other campuses and other sources to support decision-making. Assists in the development, implementation, and assessment of various survey tools including Qualtrics. Coordinates departmental and divisional reports including annual report produced in the summer, annual budget requests and manuals by gathering, updating and summarizing information. Assists in the development of presentation visuals including PowerPoint, Google Slides, Prezi, and others. Prepares and oversees the identification process and distribution of various awards for Honors Convocation and Commencement: identification and disbursement of scholarship awards (financial process), assistance with Student Affairs Awards (annual recognition process) and Alcalá Award. Prepares and oversees the identification process and communicates recommendations to Financial Aid for scholarships (Boyce, Bob Hope Leadership and Nielsen Family. Assists in responding to reports of students who may be experiencing housing insecurity.  Work closely with Case Management and other departments to ensure that we are supporting students from becoming homeless. Special Conditions of Employment: Must be able and willing to work some nights and weekends to facilitate and oversee student events. Background check: Successful completion of a pre-employment background check. Degree Verification Requirement : Persons offered employment in this position will be required to provide official education transcripts for degree verification purposes. Job Requirements: Minimum Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree required. Substitution: A High School diploma and two additional years of work experience may substitute for bachelor’s degree at the rate of one year of work experience is equivalent to two years of education. Minimum two (2) years of progressively responsible general office experience in customer, client, and or student services required. Proven skills and abilities in working with Microsoft Office suite product, especially Excel Ability to learn new software systems quickly Preferred Qualifications: Master’s degree preferred Experience working with former foster youth, formerly emancipated youth, and/or youth who may have experienced homelessness is preferred Experience working in a higher educational setting preferred Experience planning and coordinating events is preferred. Supervisory experience preferred At least 1 year of experience providing direct executive level support is preferred Performance Expectations - Knowledge, Skills and Abilities: Willingness to support the Catholic mission and vision of the University. Ability to work well independently and in a dynamic team environment, and interface well with many individuals (administrators, faculty, students and staff) using tact and diplomacy Excellent communication, customer service, and problem-solving skills Desktop computer skills, and the ability to quickly adapt to and learn new software programs Ability to work independently and effectively, as well as initiate, coordinate, oversee and complete various projects with minimal supervision Clear, concise writing skills using proper English grammar, punctuation and spelling; accurate note-taking skills including meeting minutes and proofreading skills Thorough knowledge of general office practices and procedures Basic knowledge of accounting principles Strong ability to anticipate needs and prioritize workload Must possess flexibility and versatility – able to adapt to and perform in new, changing or pressure situations Strong organizational skills with ability to handle several major projects simultaneously Ability to maintain confidentiality of information regarding students and employees as required by University policy and the Buckley Amendment Ability to create and design attractive and error free formal presentations, informational handbooks, and electronic presentations Advanced knowledge of Microsoft Suite programs including Word, Excel, and PowerPoint; familiarity with Google programs including Mail, Calendar, Sheets, Docs, and Slides Experience with social networking tools Knowledgeable with web related emerging technology and development Ability to become familiar with University history and mission, departments, fiscal procedures and specific governmental and other external regulations quickly Respond promptly to all questions and requests for information Excellent skills in the use of tools and equipment as identified below Knowledge of Workday, Banner, Cognos, Oracle, EMS, Noetix, Qualtrics, Cascade, Concur, Truescreen, Zoom, and Salesforce Demonstrated practice of centering diversity, equity, inclusion, and antiracism in one’s work including an ongoing commitment to personal learning and development in this area Demonstrated commitment to student learning and development in the co-curriculum and experience assessing the impact of programs and related services. Posting Salary: $32.58 - $36.88 per hour; Excellent Benefits The University of San Diego offers a very competitive benefits package including; medical, dental, vision, a retirement contribution given to you by the University, and access to on-campus Fitness Centers.  Please visit the benefits section of our website to view all of the perks and benefits that USD has to offer. USD: Human Resources: Benefits The salary range provided in this posting reflects what we reasonably expect to pay for this position.  Actual compensation offered or earned is dependent on experience, education and other factors including departmental budget. Special Application Instructions: Resume and Cover Letter Required Click the 'Apply Now' button to complete our online application. In addition, please upload a cover letter and resume to your application profile for the hiring managers’ review.  If you have any questions or difficulties please contact the Employment Services Team at jobs@sandiego.edu. Additional Details: Hours: 37.5 hours per week Closing date: Open until filled Note : External job postings will be up for at least three days. After that time, applications will be reviewed by the hiring manager/committee throughout the posting period. A candidate may be selected at any time which could then close this posting on a date earlier than listed. The University of San Diego is a smoking and tobacco-free campus. For more information, visit www.sandiego.edu/smokefree. Back to job search Recommended Jobs Cook San Diego, California Dining Services, Staff The University seeks an individual with a strong interest in working in a mission-driven, faith-based institution. The role of a Cook is working with the campus community and the responsibilities of the position are significantly tethered to the university’s contemporary Roman Catholic mission. Lead Cook - Asian Cuisine San Diego, California Dining Services, Staff The Lead Cook is responsible for aiding in the organization, coordination, and supervision of the preparation of hot food production, maintenance of quality and/or portion control, and the maintenance of sanitation and safety standards. The Lead Cook, under the supervision of the Chef Manager, acts as a working supervisor in training, coordinating, and evaluating the work of the lower-level cooks and other part time staff. Public Safety Officer San Diego, California Public Safety, Staff Provides a wide range of services to the University community including crime prevention, crime control, parking control and furnishing aid and information to the campus community and visitors. Facebook LinkedIn Instagram X © 2026 University of San Diego. All rights reserved. Cookie Preferences Web Privacy Web Accessibility Candidate Details We use cookies Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalise, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all"
