    "https://jobs.sandiego.edu/jobs/lead-researcher-justiceforward-initiative-san-diego-california-united-states": {
      "title": "Lead Researcher, JusticeForward Initiative",
      "description": "Lead Researcher, JusticeForward Initiative - San Diego, California, United States Skip to main content Why USD Benefits and Compensation Already Applied? My Profile Kiosk mode Employment Opportunities at USD Lead Researcher, JusticeForward Initiative San Diego, California Full-time temporary 5700 Position Title & Department: Lead Researcher, JusticeForward Initiative;  Kroc School of Peace Studies Posting # 5700 Department Description: The Kroc School of Peace Studies trains the changemakers and peacebuilders of today and tomorrow.  Kroc is the first stand-alone school of peace in the United States. Innovation in learning and programs is at the center of what we do and who we are. We combine theory and practice not just to understand crises and world issues, but also to imagine new possibilities and develop solutions to humanity’s urgent challenges from increasing inequality and violence to climate change. At Kroc, people discover new ways of building more peaceful and socially just communities through a multidisciplinary lens and experiential learning. We look for passionate individuals who believe in the power of university learning to transform the world for the better. University Description: The University of San Diego, an engaged and contemporary Catholic institution, was founded by the Diocese of San Diego and the Society of the Sacred Heart in 1949. Governed by an independent board of trustees since 1972, USD remains committed to a liberal arts education grounded in the Catholic intellectual tradition and the pursuit of truth, goodness and beauty. Inspired by this centuries old tradition of Catholic higher education, the University welcomes people of all faith traditions and any, or no, religious background. The future success of USD relies on the contributions of those who seek to foster the development of engaged global citizens and an earnest confrontation of humanity’s urgent challenges . Detailed Description: This is a full-time temporary, benefit-based position with a 30 month grant assignment. The appointment is renewable at the discretion of the University and dependent upon performance and continued funding. The Lead Researcher for the JusticeForward initiative leads applied research, student-integrated learning, and public-facing dissemination for JusticeForward: Safer Students Workforce Initiative, a grant-funded workforce development and applied research initiative designed to strengthen pathways for justice-impacted students into careers within the justice sector. Working under the direction of the Executive Director of the Kroc Institute for Peace and Justice and in close collaboration with the Director of Research, Monitoring and Evaluation, the Lead Researcher is responsible for conducting and producing the core JusticeForward research deliverables required under the grant, including a barrier and opportunity scan, learning framework, midpoint and final reports, practical public-facing resources, and related scholarly, policy, or practitioner-oriented outputs. The position integrates applied research, policy analysis, teaching, student mentorship, stakeholder engagement, and dissemination. The Lead Researcher will teach or co-teach a credit-bearing practicum course that embeds students in JusticeForward’s research, policy, and advocacy work. The position will help students contribute meaningfully to policy mapping, research brief development, stakeholder analysis, and the translation of findings into accessible practitioner-facing resources. This is a fixed-term, grant-funded position. Eighty-Five percent of the role will be assigned to JusticeForward grant activities. The remaining 15% will support other IPJ research, teaching, evaluation, program development, or applied initiatives as assigned by the Executive Director. Duties and Responsibilities: JusticeForward Applied Research and Knowledge Production Lead and manage IPJ’s JusticeForward research workstream under the direction of the Executive Director and in close coordination with the Director of Research, Monitoring and Evaluation and relevant project partners. Conduct rigorous, ethical, and well-scoped applied research using appropriate quantitative, qualitative, policy, institutional, community-engaged, or mixed-methods approaches, drawing on relevant documents, stakeholder perspectives, student learning, partner experience, and other sources of evidence. Produce grant-required and project-related research, learning, and knowledge products that are responsive to project goals, partner needs, student learning, and implementation realities, including reports, briefs, scholarly or research-oriented outputs, public-facing resources, presentations, and other applied materials as needed. Teaching, Practicum Design, and Student Mentorship Teach or co-teach credit-bearing practica that integrate students into JusticeForward research, policy, and dissemination work. Design and support structured student roles that contribute meaningfully to applied research, stakeholder engagement, practitioner-facing products, and other project needs. Mentor students engaged in JusticeForward-related research, practica, internships, presentations, or other applied learning activities, helping connect their project work to classroom learning, professional development, and justice-sector career pathways. Dissemination, Stakeholder Engagement, and Research-to-Impact Translation Translate JusticeForward research findings into clear, actionable recommendations and public-facing products for relevant policy, practitioner, community, and higher education audiences. Collaborate with IPJ communications, grant, and leadership staff to ensure that JusticeForward research products are effectively edited, designed, shared, and positioned for practical use. Help maintain, develop, and expand external relationships that support JusticeForward research, stakeholder learning, dissemination. Support stakeholder engagement, convenings, presentations, briefings, publications, and other dissemination activities connected to the JusticeForward Initiative. Broader IPJ Research, Program, and Institutional Support Contribute flexibly to other IPJ research, evaluation, teaching, public engagement, and program development activities as assigned by the Executive Director. Contribute to the growth of IPJ’s broader applied research and practice portfolio by supporting new initiatives that align with the Institute’s mission and have a plausible pathway to external support. Participate in the intellectual life of the Kroc Institute for Peace and Justice and the Kroc School through internal meetings, public events, Kroc Pot discussions, faculty/staff conversations, and other relevant activities. Other duties as assigned by the Executive Director. Special Conditions of Employment: This is a fixed-term, grant-funded position with an anticipated term of 30 months, contingent on continued grant funding and institutional approval. The role requires regular on-site presence at the University of San Diego; limited remote work may be permitted with prior approval and when compatible with business needs, but regular on-site presence is an essential expectation of the job. This role may include local travel to partner organizations, justice-sector agencies, community-based organizations, convenings, or research sites. Occasional domestic travel for conferences or professional events may be required. Background check: Successful completion of a pre-employment background check. Degree Verification Requirement : Persons offered employment in this position will be required to provide official education transcripts for degree verification purposes. Job Requirements: Minimum Qualifications: Master’s degree required in a relevant field such as peace and conflict studies, public policy, international relations, law, human rights, sociology, criminology, social work, education, political science, justice studies, public administration, or a closely related field. At least two (2) years of relevant professional experience in applied research, policy analysis, evaluation, teaching, higher education, community-engaged work, advocacy, public problem-solving, or related practice. Demonstrated ability to conduct applied research, policy analysis, evaluation, or community-engaged research, including the ability to gather, synthesize, and analyze information from documents, interviews, stakeholder consultations, literature, program data, or other relevant sources. Strong writing and analytical skills, including experience producing or contributing to research reports, policy briefs, literature reviews, evaluation products, public-facing writing, practitioner tools, grant deliverables, or other applied knowledge products. Strong organizational and project management skills, including the ability to manage multiple deadlines, coordinate across teams, and move complex projects from concept to completion. Ability to work respectfully and effectively with students, faculty, staff, community partners, public agencies, nonprofit organizations, and people with lived experience of justice-system involvement or other forms of marginalization. Demonstrated commitment to ethical, rigorous, and useful work in applied research, policy, practice, or public problem-solving. Preferred Qualifications: Terminal degree preferred, such as PhD, JD, EdD, or equivalent in a relevant field. Direct experience working on issues related to criminal justice, reentry, collateral consequences, public safety, probation, corrections, diversion, restorative justice, community violence prevention, or justice-sector workforce pathways. Familiarity with San Diego County justice-sector agencies, community-based organizations, reentry programs, or higher education programs serving justice-impacted students. Experience conducting applied research activities such as stakeholder interviews, listening sessions, focus groups, policy scans, workforce pathway mapping, institutional analysis, or implementation-oriented research. Quantitative research skills, including experience with statistics, survey design, data analysis, monitoring and evaluation systems, or mixed-methods research. Experience developing applied research products such as policy briefs, public-facing reports, pathway maps, implementation guides, practitioner tools, scholarly publications, or other products intended for academic, practitioner, policy, or community audiences. Experience teaching, co-teaching, facilitating workshops, mentoring students, supervising student researchers, or supporting credit-bearing practica, field placements, research apprenticeships, policy clinics, or other forms of practice-based learning. Demonstrated ability or strong potential to produce peer-reviewed scholarship, law review articles, book chapters, or other academically credible publications from applied research, policy research, evaluation, or community-engaged research. Performance Expectations - Knowledge, Skills and Abilities: Strong applied research and analytical skills, including the ability to organize complex information and identify practical implications for policy, practice, and institutional learning. Excellent writing and communication skills, including the ability to translate research findings into clear products for academic, practitioner, policy, community, and public audiences. Ability to collaborate effectively with students, faculty, staff, external partners, public agencies, community organizations, and people with lived experience. Strong project management skills, including the ability to manage multiple priorities, meet deadlines, coordinate deliverables, and exercise sound professional judgment. Commitment to high-quality, evidence-informed work, professional integrity, and respectful engagement with colleagues, students, partners, and external stakeholders. Posting Salary: $6,667-$7,500 per month; Excellent Benefits The University of San Diego offers a very competitive benefits package including; medical, dental, vision, a retirement contribution given to you by the University, and access to on-campus Fitness Centers.  Please visit the benefits section of our website to view all of the perks and benefits that USD has to offer. USD: Human Resources: Benefits The salary range provided in this posting reflects what we reasonably expect to pay for this position.  Actual compensation offered or earned is dependent on experience, education and other factors including departmental budget. Special Application Instructions: Resume and Cover Letter Required Click the 'Apply Now' button to complete our online application. In addition, please upload a cover letter and resume to your application profile for the hiring managers’ review.  If you have any questions or difficulties please contact the Employment Services Team at jobs@sandiego.edu. Additional Details: Hours: 37.5 hours per week Closing date: Open until filled Note : External job postings will be up for at least three days. After that time, applications will be reviewed by the hiring manager/committee throughout the posting period. A candidate may be selected at any time which could then close this posting on a date earlier than listed. The University of San Diego is a smoking and tobacco-free campus. For more information, visit www.sandiego.edu/smokefree. Back to job search Recommended Jobs Cook San Diego, California Dining Services, Staff The University seeks an individual with a strong interest in working in a mission-driven, faith-based institution. The role of a Cook is working with the campus community and the responsibilities of the position are significantly tethered to the university’s contemporary Roman Catholic mission. Lead Cook - Asian Cuisine San Diego, California Dining Services, Staff The Lead Cook is responsible for aiding in the organization, coordination, and supervision of the preparation of hot food production, maintenance of quality and/or portion control, and the maintenance of sanitation and safety standards. The Lead Cook, under the supervision of the Chef Manager, acts as a working supervisor in training, coordinating, and evaluating the work of the lower-level cooks and other part time staff. Student Life Coordinator San Diego, California Staff, Student Affairs The Student Life Coordinator provides coordination of TRS and other student facing programs and administrative support to the Assistant Vice President for Student Life. Support includes managing calendars, incoming calls, email, and written correspondence as requested. Handles highly confidential information and materials. Facebook LinkedIn Instagram X © 2026 University of San Diego. All rights reserved. Cookie Preferences Web Privacy Web Accessibility Candidate Details We use cookies Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalise, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all"
    },
    "https://jobs.sandiego.edu/jobs/project-manager-san-diego-california-united-states-da4e6a7c-02b0-4b51-a5fe-db2ba2bd9859": {
      "title": "Project Manager",
      "description": "Project Manager - San Diego, California, United States Skip to main content Why USD Benefits and Compensation Already Applied? My Profile Kiosk mode Employment Opportunities at USD Project Manager San Diego, California Full-time regular 5701 Position Title & Department: Project Manager; Planning Design and Construction Posting # 5701 Department Description: University Operations works in support of the university’s mission of advancing academic excellence, expanding liberal and professional knowledge, creating a diverse and inclusive community and preparing leaders who are dedicated to ethical conduct and compassionate service. University Description: The University of San Diego, an engaged and contemporary Catholic institution, was founded by the Diocese of San Diego and the Society of the Sacred Heart in 1949. Governed by an independent board of trustees since 1972, USD remains committed to a liberal arts education grounded in the Catholic intellectual tradition and the pursuit of truth, goodness and beauty. Inspired by this centuries old tradition of Catholic higher education, the University welcomes people of all faith traditions and any, or no, religious background. The future success of USD relies on the contributions of those who seek to foster the development of engaged global citizens and an earnest confrontation of humanity’s urgent challenges . Detailed Description: Performs skilled level work in the coordination and development of construction projects, including programming, design, university approval, construction, and move-in. Prepares and/or coordinates design packages for building interior renovations and minor new construction. Manages project contracts during the course of construction and maintains an accurate cost accounting of all construction/renovation projects. Provides construction project estimates and project schedules for all assigned projects. Supervises the maintenance of all campus architectural documents and the facilities inventory as directed by the Director - PDC. Duties and Responsibilities: Pre-Construction/Design Interviews university personnel and ascertains user renovation/new construction needs. Translates user needs into acceptable construction documents in accordance with current building code and ADA regulations. Verifies and documents existing site conditions prior to beginning design. Prepares and/or coordinates drawings and specifications for all assigned renovation and construction projects. Prepares and/or coordinates building elevations and renderings as required. Revises construction documents as necessary after change order approval. If using outside design firm, verifies that submitted design documents conform to USD design standards, tenant/client needs and all applicable codes. Strives to create sustainable designs, targeting LEED Gold criteria when possible. Prepares detailed cost estimates for early budget and funding needs. Construction Management Responsible for the management of interior renovation projects and minor new construction projects in terms of budget, schedule, and quality of workmanship as directed by the Director of PDC. Issues contracts and purchase orders to contractors and/or vendors. Ensures that all construction is completed in accordance with current building, electrical, and mechanical codes, as well as with the approved design. Coordinates with the City of San Diego on all permit related issues. Estimate construction costs to establish preliminary budgets. Maintains project budgets as directed by the Director.  Project budgets will range and may be $20M for a single capital project. Estimates cost of changes requested by the user. Continues interaction with the user(s) during construction to ensure approved changes are accomplished. Coordinates project approval with appropriate University personnel. Coordinates project construction/completion/move-in with appropriate University departments. As directed by the Director of PDC, develops and manages special projects pertaining to university facilities. Office Management Assists in maintaining the archived drawings and specifications. Keeps the Director of PDC appraised of project(s) conditions. Safety Consistently follows safety practices as appropriate to the trade and complies with rules and regulations established by the university, state of California, and federal agencies. Follows established procedures for dealing with special hazards and promptly reports any on the job accidents to Public Safety and to your chain of command and to the AVP of Facilities Management in writing. Maintains an awareness of the typical hazards of the work place as well as special hazards that may be encountered at any university facility location such as biohazards, low-grade radiation and chemicals known to the State of California to cause cancer or birth defects. Certificates, Licenses, Registrations: Valid California Driver’s License required. Special Conditions of Employment: May be required to drive university vehicles. Background check: Successful completion of a pre-employment background check. Degree Verification Requirement : Persons offered employment in this position will be required to provide official education transcripts for degree verification purposes. Job Requirements: Minimum Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree required, preferably in architecture, construction management, engineering or a related field. Substitution : A High school diploma and additional years of relevant work experience may substitute for the education requirement, at the rate of two years of experience is equivalent to one year of education. Three (3) years of experience managing commercial or institutional construction projects required. Preferred Qualifications: Prefer to have three (3) years of experience in drafting construction documents in a commercial or institutional setting. Performance Expectations - Knowledge, Skills and Abilities: Must be able to convert user concepts into project documents. Must be able to communicate effectively both orally and in writing. Must have a thorough knowledge of construction materials and methods. Must be able to manage multiple construction projects including making decisions concerning scope of work and costs. Must have a thorough knowledge of current Uniform Building, Fire, and Mechanical Codes as well as the National Electric Code and ADA regulations. Must have the ability to follow directions. Must be physically able to consistently perform job duties. Must have the ability to maintain a professional working relationship with students, faculty, and staff. Must adhere to University policies and procedures. Must have the ability to supervise and instruct assigned employees. Must possess a general knowledge of construction law. Must be able to demonstrate advanced ability with the following software: such as Microsoft Project, Microsoft Office Suite, Bluebeam Studio, Document Storage sites.  Prefer experience with the following software: AutoCAD, TMA/Maximo, Aperture, Photoshop, Construction management software such as EBuilder, ProLog, etc. Posting Salary: $8,750-$9,583.33 per month; Excellent Benefits The University of San Diego offers a very competitive benefits package including; medical, dental, vision, a retirement contribution given to you by the University, and access to on-campus Fitness Centers.  Please visit the benefits section of our website to view all of the perks and benefits that USD has to offer. USD: Human Resources: Benefits The salary range provided in this posting reflects what we reasonably expect to pay for this position.  Actual compensation offered or earned is dependent on experience, education and other factors including departmental budget. Special Application Instructions: Resume Required Click the 'Apply Now' button to complete our online application and, for full consideration, please upload a resume to your application profile for the hiring managers’ review.  If you have any questions or difficulties please contact the Employment Services Team at jobs@sandiego.edu. Additional Details: Hours: 40 hours per week Closing date: Open until filled Note : External job postings will be up for at least three days. After that time, applications will be reviewed by the hiring manager/committee throughout the posting period. A candidate may be selected at any time which could then close this posting on a date earlier than listed. The University of San Diego is a smoking and tobacco-free campus. For more information, visit www.sandiego.edu/smokefree. Back to job search Recommended Jobs Cook San Diego, California Dining Services, Staff The University seeks an individual with a strong interest in working in a mission-driven, faith-based institution. The role of a Cook is working with the campus community and the responsibilities of the position are significantly tethered to the university’s contemporary Roman Catholic mission. Lead Cook - Asian Cuisine San Diego, California Dining Services, Staff The Lead Cook is responsible for aiding in the organization, coordination, and supervision of the preparation of hot food production, maintenance of quality and/or portion control, and the maintenance of sanitation and safety standards. The Lead Cook, under the supervision of the Chef Manager, acts as a working supervisor in training, coordinating, and evaluating the work of the lower-level cooks and other part time staff. Student Life Coordinator San Diego, California Staff, Student Affairs The Student Life Coordinator provides coordination of TRS and other student facing programs and administrative support to the Assistant Vice President for Student Life. Support includes managing calendars, incoming calls, email, and written correspondence as requested. Handles highly confidential information and materials. Facebook LinkedIn Instagram X © 2026 University of San Diego. All rights reserved. Cookie Preferences Web Privacy Web Accessibility Candidate Details We use cookies Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalise, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all"
