    "https://jobs.sandiego.edu/jobs/assistant-director-of-outreach-and-recruitment-san-diego-california-united-states-752d9ce8-c8fb-4c89-a337-ce4caceaf0a4": {
      "title": "Assistant Director of Outreach and Recruitment",
      "description": "Assistant Director of Outreach and Recruitment - San Diego, California, United States Skip to main content Why USD Benefits and Compensation Already Applied? My Profile Kiosk mode Employment Opportunities at USD Assistant Director of Outreach and Recruitment San Diego, California Full-time regular 5765 Position Title & Department: Assistant Director of Outreach and Recruitment; School of Peace Studies\n\n\nPosting # 5765 Department Description:\nThe Kroc School of Peace Studies trains the changemakers and peacebuilders of today and tomorrow.  Kroc is the first stand-alone school of peace in the United States. Innovation in learning and programs is at the center of what we do and who we are. We combine theory and practice not just to understand crises and world issues, but also to imagine new possibilities and develop solutions to humanity’s urgent challenges from increasing inequality and violence to climate change. At Kroc, people discover new ways of building more peaceful and socially just communities through a multidisciplinary lens and experiential learning. We look for passionate individuals who believe in the power of university learning to transform the world for the better.\n\n\nUniversity Description: The University of San Diego, an engaged and contemporary Catholic institution, was founded by the Diocese of San Diego and the Society of the Sacred Heart in 1949. Governed by an independent board of trustees since 1972, USD remains committed to a liberal arts education grounded in the Catholic intellectual tradition and the pursuit of truth, goodness and beauty. Inspired by this centuries old tradition of Catholic higher education, the University welcomes people of all faith traditions and any, or no, religious background. The future success of USD relies on the contributions of those who seek to foster the development of engaged global citizens and an earnest confrontation of humanity’s urgent challenges . Detailed Description: The Assistant Director of Outreach and Recruitment plays a key role in advancing the Kroc School’s strategic enrollment goals across its portfolio of graduate, certificate, and other academic programs. Working collaboratively with the Director of Recruitment and Admissions, the central Enrollment Management team, and other internal and external partners, the Assistant Director engages and advises prospective students, supports admissions and enrollment activities, coordinates recruitment initiatives and events, and helps admitted students progress toward enrollment. The position uses enrollment data, CRM systems, and prospective student feedback to prioritize outreach, monitor engagement, and recommend adjustments to recruitment and admissions activities. Responsibilities and areas of emphasis may shift throughout the enrollment cycle based on program priorities, enrollment goals, staffing needs, and broader business needs. This role requires a proactive, collaborative, and customer-focused professional who can build relationships with diverse populations, manage multiple priorities, and provide prospective and admitted students with a timely, personalized, and well-coordinated experience. This role requires regular local and regional travel, periodic overnight and out-of-area travel, and some evening and weekend work based on recruitment and event schedules. Duties and Responsibilities: Recruitment and Admissions Prospective Student Engagement and Recruitment Engage prospective, applicant, admitted, and enrolling students through personalized outreach, advising appointments, information sessions, campus visits, and other virtual and in-person interactions. Manage assigned prospective student populations, academic programs, recruitment sources, or stages of the enrollment funnel. Assignments may be adjusted based on enrollment priorities and departmental needs. Implement established recruitment and lead-engagement strategies using phone, email, text, video, virtual meetings, and other approved communication channels. Prioritize outreach based on prospective student engagement, recruitment source, enrollment stage, program needs, and available enrollment data. Provide accurate and timely information about academic programs, admissions requirements, application processes, deadlines, financial resources, and enrollment next steps. Refer complex or specialized questions to the appropriate school or university representative. Maintain timely and consistent follow-up with prospective students and identify questions, concerns, and barriers that may affect their decision to apply or enroll. Admissions and Enrollment Support Support students as they progress through the admissions and enrollment process, including application completion, admission, confirmation, and matriculation. Conduct individual admissions advising appointments and provide personalized guidance appropriate to the students’ stage in the enrollment process. Develop and implement recruitment and yield activities designed to increase prospective student engagement, applications, confirmations, and enrollment. Engage admitted students through individual outreach, events, and other activities designed to answer questions, address barriers, and support informed enrollment decisions. Coordinate with the Director of Recruitment and Admissions and other appropriate university offices to address questions related to scholarships, financial aid, academic programs, international requirements, student services, and enrollment. Provide coverage and support across recruitment and admissions functions as needed to maintain service continuity and respond to changing enrollment and departmental priorities. Recruitment Events and Outreach Plan, coordinate, host, and represent the Kroc School at recruitment activities, including information sessions, open houses, campus visits, graduate fairs, conferences, webinars, community events, and other virtual or in-person programs. Manage assigned event logistics, communications, prospective student follow-up, and outcome tracking in collaboration with internal and external partners. Travel regularly to represent the Kroc School at local and regional recruitment activities, with periodic overnight and out-of-area travel. Work evenings and weekends as required by recruitment, travel, and event schedules. Partnership Development Develop and maintain recruitment relationships with colleges and universities, employers, professional associations, community organizations, military-affiliated populations, student organizations, alumni, and other partners that can support enrollment goals. Identify and recommend new outreach opportunities, partnerships, events, and recruitment channels based on program priorities and prospective student needs. Engage faculty, alumni, current students, and student ambassadors in recruitment and yield activities, including events, panels, prospective student conversations, testimonials, and other engagement opportunities. CRM, Data, and Continuous Improvement Maintain accurate and timely records of prospective student interactions, recruitment activities, outcomes, and next steps in the school’s CRM and other designated systems. Monitor and review recruitment and enrollment activity, lead engagement, funnel movement, event participation, conversion patterns, and other relevant enrollment indicators. Prepare regular activity and outcome reports and recommend tactical adjustments based on recruitment results, prospective student feedback, market conditions, and enrollment priorities. Support the implementation, testing, adoption, and continuous improvement of CRM, admissions, communication, and reporting systems. Contribute to the development, documentation, and refinement of recruitment and admissions processes, communication plans, and operating procedures. Collaboration Collaborate with the central Enrollment Management team, Marketing, academic programs, faculty, program directors, university offices, and other partners to ensure that recruitment information and prospective student communications are accurate, timely, and aligned with school priorities. Share prospective student feedback, recruitment trends, and relevant enrollment information with appropriate stakeholders. Participate in departmental and cross-functional meetings and contribute to the planning and implementation of recruitment and admissions priorities. Special Projects and Other Duties Lead or collaborate in special projects, pilot initiatives, and other assignments that advance the Kroc School’s enrollment priorities, operational needs, and strategic goals. Perform other related duties as needed and assigned. Background check: Successful completion of a pre-employment background check. Degree Verification Requirement : Persons offered employment in this position will be required to provide official education transcripts for degree verification purposes. Job Requirements: Minimum Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree from an accredited college or university required. Minimum three years of progressively responsible experience in higher education admissions, student recruitment, enrollment management, consultative sales, marketing, or a related field. Experience providing individualized guidance or customer service and managing ongoing relationships with prospective students, clients, or comparable populations. Experience using a CRM or another system to manage communications, activity, records, and outcomes. Preferred Qualifications: Master’s degree in higher education administration, business, marketing, peace and justice, or a related field preferred. Experience recruiting for graduate or certificate programs is preferred. Experience developing and implementing recruitment strategies, outreach initiatives, or partnership activities preferred. Experience planning and executing recruitment events, conferences, campus visits, or similar programs is preferred. Experience using HubSpot, Slate, Salesforce, or a comparable CRM preferred. Experience working with military-affiliated students or other targeted recruitment populations preferred. Experience using recruitment and enrollment data to inform outreach and engagement activities preferred. Performance Expectations - Knowledge, Skills and Abilities: Strong commitment to the mission and values of the Kroc School and the University of San Diego. Excellent interpersonal, listening, written, and verbal communication skills. Demonstrated ability to build relationships with individuals from diverse backgrounds, nationalities, identities, experiences, and abilities. Demonstrated ability to interface and collaborate effectively with central university functions, specifically the central Enrollment Management team, to align school-level efforts with overarching university enrollment strategies. Ability to conduct effective virtual and in-person admissions advising. Ability to manage a portfolio of prospective and admitted students while meeting established outreach, service, and response expectations. Demonstrated reliability, responsiveness, initiative, and follow-through. Strong organizational and time-management skills, with the ability to manage multiple priorities, projects, events, and deadlines. Ability to work independently while collaborating effectively within a diverse team. Ability to maintain accurate records and consistently document activity in CRM and admissions systems. Ability to interpret recruitment and enrollment data, identify trends, and recommend improvements. Proficiency with CRM systems, database tools, spreadsheets, word-processing applications, virtual meeting platforms, and other web-based technology. Experience in project management and event planning. Exceptional attention to detail and sound professional judgment. Ability to handle confidential and sensitive information appropriately. Knowledge of higher education policies and procedures, including FERPA requirements. Ability to adapt responsibilities and priorities in response to changing enrollment goals and business needs. Posting Salary: $5,859-$6,613 per month; Excellent Benefits The University of San Diego offers a very competitive benefits package including; medical, dental, vision, a retirement contribution given to you by the University, and access to on-campus Fitness Centers.  Please visit the benefits section of our website to view all of the perks and benefits that USD has to offer. USD: Human Resources: Benefits The salary range provided in this posting reflects what we reasonably expect to pay for this position.  Actual compensation offered or earned is dependent on experience, education and other factors including departmental budget. Special Application Instructions: Resume and Cover Letter Required Click the 'Apply Now' button to complete our online application. In addition, please upload a cover letter and resume to your application profile for the hiring managers’ review.  If you have any questions or difficulties please contact the Employment Services Team at jobs@sandiego.edu. Additional Details: Hours: 37.5 per hour Closing date: Open until filled Note : External job postings will be up for at least three days. After that time, applications will be reviewed by the hiring manager/committee throughout the posting period. A candidate may be selected at any time which could then close this posting on a date earlier than listed. The University of San Diego is a smoking and tobacco-free campus. For more information, visit www.sandiego.edu/smokefree. Recommended Jobs Career Readiness Program Manager San Diego, California Office of Career Services, Staff The Career Readiness Program Manager is responsible for the operational administration, student engagement, and delivery of the Career Readiness Program (CRP - comprising PASSPORT, COMPASS, and CONNECT), a graduation requirement for undergraduate students within the College of Arts and Sciences, the Knauss School of Business, and the Shiley-Marcos School of Engineering. Administrative Director, Consumer Protection Policy Center San Diego, California Administrator, School of Law Under the general direction of the Executive Director of the Centers for Public Interest Law (CPIL) and Consumer Protection Policy Center (CPPC), and reporting to the Children’s Advocacy Institute’s (CAI) Administrative Director, this position is responsible for administrative functions, academic-related functions, and policy-related functions of CPPC. This position coordinates with the directors of CPPC’s two sister organizations at CPIL — CAI and the Energy Policy Initiatives Center (EPIC); CPIL’s Associate Director; and the CPIL/CPPC Executive Director, who oversees all academic instruction of law students enrolled in CPPC courses and clinics. This position also works closely with leadership and colleagues at the USD School of Law and interacts with various offices across the USD campus. Product Marketing Specialist San Diego, California Joan B. Kroc School of Peace Studies, Staff The Product Marketing Specialist (Specialist) is responsible for the market positioning and lead generation strategies of the Kroc School of Peace Studies academic programs portfolio. The Specialist manages processes and initiatives across multiple platforms to increase student enrollment, program development, and tuition revenue in a supportive environment, while positively shaping the Kroc School’s identity, profile, and visibility within the community. Facebook LinkedIn Instagram X © 2026 University of San Diego. All rights reserved. Cookie Preferences Web Privacy Web Accessibility Candidate Details We use cookies Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalise, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all"
    },
    "https://jobs.sandiego.edu/jobs/career-readiness-program-manager-san-diego-california-united-states": {
      "title": "Career Readiness Program Manager",
      "description": "Career Readiness Program Manager - San Diego, California, United States Skip to main content Why USD Benefits and Compensation Already Applied? My Profile Kiosk mode Employment Opportunities at USD Career Readiness Program Manager San Diego, California Full-time regular 5763 Position Title & Department: Career Readiness Program Manager; Career Development Center\n\n\nPosting # 5763 Department Description: The Career Development Center supports students in developing and achieving career goals that lead to meaningful lives. University Description: The University of San Diego, an engaged and contemporary Catholic institution, was founded by the Diocese of San Diego and the Society of the Sacred Heart in 1949. Governed by an independent board of trustees since 1972, USD remains committed to a liberal arts education grounded in the Catholic intellectual tradition and the pursuit of truth, goodness and beauty. Inspired by this centuries old tradition of Catholic higher education, the University welcomes people of all faith traditions and any, or no, religious background. The future success of USD relies on the contributions of those who seek to foster the development of engaged global citizens and an earnest confrontation of humanity’s urgent challenges . Detailed Description: The Career Readiness Program Manager is responsible for the operational administration, student engagement, and delivery of the Career Readiness Program (CRP - comprising PASSPORT, COMPASS, and CONNECT), a graduation requirement for undergraduate students within the College of Arts and Sciences, the Knauss School of Business, and the Shiley-Marcos School of Engineering. Managing the student CRP onboarding process, the Manager executes onboarding activities and targeted outreach efforts to guide undergraduates through required program milestones from initial enrollment toward graduation and meaningful professional futures. This role serves as a functional liaison to academic departments, advisors, and student support staff to facilitate CRP workflows across campus. Duties and Responsibilities: Career Readiness Program Lifecycle Execution Manages the student onboarding lifecycle pipeline and continuous engagement within the Career Readiness Program (CRP - PASSPORT, COMPASS, and CONNECT), maintaining tracking checkpoints to monitor and advance students through required milestones. Facilitates the delivery of all Career Readiness Program (CRP) orientation sessions, running virtual, in-person, first-year, and transfer student onboarding initiatives to establish early program momentum. Execute operational and administrative workflows for program management and backend tracking systems, collaborating with the Registrar's Office and IT to ensure student data and quality control procedures are configured accurately across program tracking platforms. Manages the end-to-end vetting, approval, and data workflows for campus-partner CRP event submissions; consults with campus partners on program alignment, utilizing Salesforce to award points and ensure accurate tracking of student program milestones. Processes and evaluates specialized CRP point requests, including the review and verification of leadership and internship reflections, to validate CRP point eligibility. Facilitates backend verification and administrative workflows for specialized experiential learning and off-campus practical experience options, partnering with campus stakeholders to ensure seamless cross-departmental processing. Executes proactive onboarding and scalable, milestone-based outreach campaigns throughout the student CRP lifecycle, utilizing data insights to target specific student cohorts and support continuous progress toward program completion Collaborate with the Director of Career Development and Readiness and campus partners to explore, design, and implement solutions to improve CRP workflows and processes Serves as the primary CRP operational liaison to campus partners, academic advisors, and frontline student support staff - collaborating to integrate milestones into departmental workflows, deliver resources, and review tracking data to address engagement gaps for targeted student populations (such as transfer, non-traditional, and commuter students). Career Readiness Student Engagement Provides targeted CRP guidance and milestone support for students, utilizing proactive outreach to assist with technical program requirements and ensure purposeful completion. Delivers specialized, high-impact CRP workshops and classroom presentations - including weekly CRP orientations and transfer orientations - collaborating with campus partners to expand delivery and drive student engagement across designated academic departments. Collaborates cross-functionally with colleagues and the communications team to synchronize early-engagement messaging and integrate CRP touchpoints into key milestone campus events. Assists with the planning, execution, and support of broader departmental programming, events, and initiatives as required. Supervision and Career Development Support Supervises and coordinates daily assignments for student employees or interns supporting CRP operations. Collaborates across the department to monitor student CRP progression metrics and gather team feedback to optimize program delivery. Participates in departmental planning and committee initiatives to support CRP goals and enhance program impact. Identifies opportunities for process improvement, resource expansion, and increased student participation in the CRP. Onboards and helps train new staff members on policies and procedures related to the Career Readiness Program. Serves as liaison to professional associations to stay abreast of current developments in career development and readiness trends. Background check: Successful completion of a pre-employment background check. Degree Verification Requirement : Persons offered employment in this position will be required to provide official education transcripts for degree verification purposes. Job Requirements: Minimum Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree. Substitution: A master’s degree may substitute for one year of experience, at the rate of two years of higher education is equivalent to one year of work experience. 2 years of related work experience required. Preferred Qualifications: Prior experience working directly with college students within higher education. Experience managing, organizing, and maintaining large data sets. Experience utilizing Salesforce, Banner, or equivalent CRM and higher education database systems. Proven presentation and public speaking skills, including workshop or group facilitation. Demonstrated experience working in cross-functional teams. Strong customer service background with a demonstrated ability to triage inquiries, resolve complex issues, and guide students effectively. Performance Expectations - Knowledge, Skills and Abilities: High level of accuracy and strong attention to detail in managing data, updating records, and tracking program milestones. Ability to manage Career Readiness Program (CRP) operations, tracking progression checkpoints for undergraduate student cohorts. Ability to execute targeted outreach campaigns to engage specific student cohorts and support continuous program completion. Professional demeanor and superior interpersonal skills, with the ability to build collaborative working relationships with staff and campus partners. Ability to deliver engaging career readiness programming and orientations to undergraduate populations. Effective oral and written communication skills, including the ability to facilitate orientation sessions and present information clearly to students and campus partners. Knowledge of NACE (National Association of Colleges and Employers) career readiness competencies and the ability to integrate them into programmatic initiatives. Knowledge of Salesforce or specialized CRMs, with the ability to input data, track student metrics, and maintain data integrity. Ability to process engagement tracking workflows and review student records to support timely program tracking. Ability to effectively guide, mentor, and assign daily tasks to student employees and student assistants. Ability to work in a fast-paced environment, managing multiple priorities with strong organizational skills. Ability to support the University’s mission by identifying operational and process improvements to promote equitable program access. Posting Salary: $28.36 - $36.82 Per Hour; Excellent Benefits The University of San Diego offers a very competitive benefits package including; medical, dental, vision, a retirement contribution given to you by the University, and access to on-campus Fitness Centers.  Please visit the benefits section of our website to view all of the perks and benefits that USD has to offer. USD: Human Resources: Benefits The salary range provided in this posting reflects what we reasonably expect to pay for this position.  Actual compensation offered or earned is dependent on experience, education and other factors including departmental budget. Special Application Instructions: Resume and Cover Letter Required Click the 'Apply Now' button to complete our online application. In addition, please upload a cover letter and resume to your application profile for the hiring managers’ review.  If you have any questions or difficulties please contact the Employment Services Team at jobs@sandiego.edu. Additional Details: Hours: 37.5 hours per week Closing date: Open until filled Note : External job postings will be up for at least three days. After that time, applications will be reviewed by the hiring manager/committee throughout the posting period. A candidate may be selected at any time which could then close this posting on a date earlier than listed. The University of San Diego is a smoking and tobacco-free campus. For more information, visit www.sandiego.edu/smokefree. Recommended Jobs Assistant Director of Outreach and Recruitment San Diego, California Administrator, Joan B. Kroc School of Peace Studies The Assistant Director of Outreach and Recruitment plays a key role in advancing the Kroc\nSchool’s strategic enrollment goals across its portfolio of graduate, certificate, and other\nacademic programs. Working collaboratively with the Director of Recruitment and Admissions,\nthe central Enrollment Management team, and other internal and external partners, the\nAssistant Director engages and advises prospective students, supports admissions and\nenrollment activities, coordinates recruitment initiatives and events, and helps admitted students progress toward enrollment. Administrative Director, Consumer Protection Policy Center San Diego, California Administrator, School of Law Under the general direction of the Executive Director of the Centers for Public Interest Law (CPIL) and Consumer Protection Policy Center (CPPC), and reporting to the Children’s Advocacy Institute’s (CAI) Administrative Director, this position is responsible for administrative functions, academic-related functions, and policy-related functions of CPPC. This position coordinates with the directors of CPPC’s two sister organizations at CPIL — CAI and the Energy Policy Initiatives Center (EPIC); CPIL’s Associate Director; and the CPIL/CPPC Executive Director, who oversees all academic instruction of law students enrolled in CPPC courses and clinics. This position also works closely with leadership and colleagues at the USD School of Law and interacts with various offices across the USD campus. Product Marketing Specialist San Diego, California Joan B. Kroc School of Peace Studies, Staff The Product Marketing Specialist (Specialist) is responsible for the market positioning and lead generation strategies of the Kroc School of Peace Studies academic programs portfolio. The Specialist manages processes and initiatives across multiple platforms to increase student enrollment, program development, and tuition revenue in a supportive environment, while positively shaping the Kroc School’s identity, profile, and visibility within the community. Facebook LinkedIn Instagram X © 2026 University of San Diego. All rights reserved. Cookie Preferences Web Privacy Web Accessibility Candidate Details We use cookies Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalise, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all"
    },
    "https://jobs.sandiego.edu/jobs/retail-store-manager-san-diego-california-united-states-6abdf107-4cdb-428c-bb47-085f1f1cf5f1": {
      "title": "Retail Store Manager",
      "description": "Retail Store Manager - San Diego, California, United States Skip to main content Why USD Benefits and Compensation Already Applied? My Profile Kiosk mode Employment Opportunities at USD Retail Store Manager San Diego, California Full-time regular 5773 Position Title & Department: Retail Store Manager; Torero Store\n\n\nPosting # 5773 Department Description: The USD Torero Store, located in the Hahn University Center, serves as the University of San Diego's primary campus retail hub. This store provides students, faculty, staff, alumni, and visitors with USD-branded apparel, gifts, course materials, technology, and everyday campus essentials. Operating under Auxiliary Services, the Torero Store team is dedicated to delivering exceptional customer service, engaging campus promotions, and a welcoming retail experience that reflects the spirit of the university. University Description: The University of San Diego, an engaged and contemporary Catholic institution, was founded by the Diocese of San Diego and the Society of the Sacred Heart in 1949. Governed by an independent board of trustees since 1972, USD remains committed to a liberal arts education grounded in the Catholic intellectual tradition and the pursuit of truth, goodness and beauty. Inspired by this centuries old tradition of Catholic higher education, the University welcomes people of all faith traditions and any, or no, religious background. The future success of USD relies on the contributions of those who seek to foster the development of engaged global citizens and an earnest confrontation of humanity’s urgent challenges . Detailed Description: The Retail Store Manager oversees retail operations for the USD Torero Store and is responsible for apparel, emblematic, gift merchandise planning and forecasting of sales. The Retail Store Manager supervises, trains and assists employees like sales representatives and retail store specialists in customer service, store maintenance and product promotions. Develop and maintain a schedule for employees and promotions centered on special sales events and other cycles. Seeks ways to better promote the store, the product line and services within the store. Maintain proper inventory levels, ensure stocking, implement purchasing plans and maintain contact with suppliers to ensure maximum efficiency in meeting sales goals. Implement cross-training of employees to always maintain productivity. Manage all controllable costs with a view to maintain profitability. Ensure the store always remains clean and presentable. Hire and train as needed to ensure adequate personnel is on hand to provide outstanding customer services. Responsible for timely and accurate POS data entry and maintenance. Periodically, this position may be asked to contribute to either ad hoc or ongoing division/campus projects outside the scope of their day-to-day activities. Duties and Responsibilities: Supervision/Leadership • Exercise administrative supervision over student sales associates and assigned staff. • Assist with development and implementation of sales associate work schedules. • Serves as a leader for employees while fostering teamwork, employee morale, motivation, and open communication. • Understands and assures compliance with all USD Torero Store, USD Licensing, Auxiliary Services and University of San Diego policies and procedures • Conducts new employee orientation for apparel and emblematic merchandise, sales floor, and back-stock storage organization • Be an example and teacher of non-negotiable standards of performance to sales associates and assigned staff. • Set leadership example by a willingness to work all job tasks. • Provide opportunities for sales associates development through mentorships and internships • Delegate assignments as appropriate and follows up to ensure that work is accurate and complete • Responsible for the smooth flow of work, communication, and ideas through a congenial, caring, and supportive attitude • Takes corrective action to ensure acceptable employee performance and provides both positive and corrective feedback to employees regarding performance. • Represent the USD Torero Store at off-site locations in a professional manner. Attend trade shows and educational seminars as assigned. • Monitor customer service delivered by student sales associate employees to maintain superior customer service levels. Apparel Fashion, Emblematic, Gift Buying • Plans, forecasts, and initiates purchasing for apparel, emblematic and all other assigned merchandise categories. • Meets with vendors on a regular basis. • Coordinates with Licensing Director on licensing promotions, vendor compliance and other projects as needed. • Evaluates pricing, buying group data, terms, freight, discounts, co-op, and markdown dollars, return policies, brand identification, and representation. • Responsible for updating products and pricing in POS and on e-commerce site, updating, and modifying descriptions, and other technology systems on a continual basis • All sales, margins, etc. reviewed weekly with Director to determine adherence to gross margin goal objectives. Maintains appropriate inventory levels to achieve sales and service goals. • Assure adherence to all USD branding and graphic standards. • Promote and adhere to USD licensing standards and protocol. • Create, market, and maintain apparel and emblematic assortments for departmental and student organization purchase. • Stay abreast of merchandise trends; continually seek out new vendors and products. • Maintain delivery calendar for peak times in conjunction with all Torero Store departments. • Creatively merchandise in Torero Store and other auxiliary units as needed. Operations/Financial Management • Conducts weekly analysis of inventory levels and sales trends. • Merchandise sales floor to ensure that all product is stocked, clean and displayed to maximize sales. Rotate sales floor on a continual basis. Ensures inventory on sales floor at levels allowing maximized sales momentum and shopablity. • All purchase orders to be entered accurately, completely, and timely. • Plans promotions, sales, and special events across campus. Coordinate with other store departments as needed, as well as other departments of the university on a regular basis. • Maintains accurate inventory management system, including all markdowns, markups, special promotions, and inventory transfers between units. • Meets with Director weekly to ensure all service levels operate at optimum, to evaluate revenue, expenses, and promotions • Assist in planning and implementation of employee meetings. • Assist in preparing Merchandise Department for annual physical inventory. • Share in operational management of sales floor, opening and closing of store, daily cash deposits, and student sales associate supervision General Duties • Manage store as needed (evenings, weekends, and holidays) • Participates in administrative staff meetings and assist in long-range unit planning activities. • Assists with establishing and maintaining departmental goals. • Prepares report as required. • Strives to improve and streamline departmental operations through the continuous assessment of guidelines and procedures, work processes and program effectiveness/value. • Participates in professional organizations, conferences and training activities, representing the department as required or assigned. • Some travel required. • Able to operate Torero Store equipment and train others on proper use Special Conditions of Employment: Must be able to work a varied work schedule including evenings, weekends, and holidays. Must be able to work a flexible schedule to accommodate Fall and Spring Back-to-School seasons, special events and annual inventory. Background check: Successful completion of a pre-employment background check. Degree Verification Requirement : Persons offered employment in this position will be required to provide official education transcripts for degree verification purposes. Job Requirements: Minimum Qualifications: Bachelor's degree required. Related experience may substitute for the education requirement at the rate of one year of experience equals two years of education (in addition to the five years of experience). At least 5 years of retail management experience. At least 5 years of apparel/emblematic/git etc. buying in retail industry. Demonstrated knowledge of open to buy, inventory management, gross margin calculations, turns and other financial considerations for a profitable department. Knowledge of graphics, emblematic product, and apparel trends. Demonstrated knowledge of principles, techniques, practices, and procedures in retail, purchasing and sales. Demonstrated knowledge of supervisory techniques and employee development and evaluation. Demonstrated competency with computer applications, including Google Suite and Microsoft Office suite. (Adobe Photoshop or similar applications preferred.) Demonstrated knowledge of merchandising skills across diverse retail products. Demonstrated knowledge of website management systems. Knowledge of POS and inventory management systems. Demonstrated knowledge of retailing trends. Ability to design cutting edge campus apparel. Performance Expectations - Knowledge, Skills and Abilities: Ability to make appropriate buying decisions, sustaining inventory based on financial purchasing principles, including markdowns, sales goals, forecasting, gross margins, etc. Ability to develop and implement marketing strategies and goals. Ability to exercise independent judgment. Excellent problem-solving skills, as well as attention to detail and ability to prioritize tasks in a fast-paced environment. Excellent oral, written and interpersonal skills. Ability to merchandise a wide array of product in an attractive, clean, uncluttered and creative fashion. Ability to update products and pricing on e-commerce site. Posting Salary: $5,858.67 - $6,116.67 Per month; Excellent Benefits The University of San Diego offers a very competitive benefits package including; medical, dental, vision, a retirement contribution given to you by the University, and access to on-campus Fitness Centers.  Please visit the benefits section of our website to view all of the perks and benefits that USD has to offer. USD: Human Resources: Benefits The salary range provided in this posting reflects what we reasonably expect to pay for this position.  Actual compensation offered or earned is dependent on experience, education and other factors including departmental budget. Special Application Instructions: Resume and Cover Letter Required Click the 'Apply Now' button to complete our online application. In addition, please upload a cover letter and resume to your application profile for the hiring managers’ review.  If you have any questions or difficulties please contact the Employment Services Team at jobs@sandiego.edu. Additional Details: Hours: 40 hours per week Closing date: Open until filled Note : External job postings will be up for at least three days. After that time, applications will be reviewed by the hiring manager/committee throughout the posting period. A candidate may be selected at any time which could then close this posting on a date earlier than listed. The University of San Diego is a smoking and tobacco-free campus. For more information, visit www.sandiego.edu/smokefree. Recommended Jobs Assistant Director of Outreach and Recruitment San Diego, California Administrator, Joan B. Kroc School of Peace Studies The Assistant Director of Outreach and Recruitment plays a key role in advancing the Kroc\nSchool’s strategic enrollment goals across its portfolio of graduate, certificate, and other\nacademic programs. Working collaboratively with the Director of Recruitment and Admissions,\nthe central Enrollment Management team, and other internal and external partners, the\nAssistant Director engages and advises prospective students, supports admissions and\nenrollment activities, coordinates recruitment initiatives and events, and helps admitted students progress toward enrollment. Career Readiness Program Manager San Diego, California Office of Career Services, Staff The Career Readiness Program Manager is responsible for the operational administration, student engagement, and delivery of the Career Readiness Program (CRP - comprising PASSPORT, COMPASS, and CONNECT), a graduation requirement for undergraduate students within the College of Arts and Sciences, the Knauss School of Business, and the Shiley-Marcos School of Engineering. Administrative Director, Consumer Protection Policy Center San Diego, California Administrator, School of Law Under the general direction of the Executive Director of the Centers for Public Interest Law (CPIL) and Consumer Protection Policy Center (CPPC), and reporting to the Children’s Advocacy Institute’s (CAI) Administrative Director, this position is responsible for administrative functions, academic-related functions, and policy-related functions of CPPC. This position coordinates with the directors of CPPC’s two sister organizations at CPIL — CAI and the Energy Policy Initiatives Center (EPIC); CPIL’s Associate Director; and the CPIL/CPPC Executive Director, who oversees all academic instruction of law students enrolled in CPPC courses and clinics. This position also works closely with leadership and colleagues at the USD School of Law and interacts with various offices across the USD campus. Facebook LinkedIn Instagram X © 2026 University of San Diego. All rights reserved. Cookie Preferences Web Privacy Web Accessibility Candidate Details We use cookies Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalise, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all"
